        <merged-without-qudt-and-inferred:has_doi rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">10.1016/j.apsusc.2023.157330</merged-without-qudt-and-inferred:has_doi>
        <merged-without-qudt-and-inferred:has_title rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Constructing the active pairs of Co0-Co2+ via surface solid reaction on TiO2 with the additives of La and Al for higher alcohols synthesis</merged-without-qudt-and-inferred:has_title>
        <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Abstract:The conversion of syngas (CO + H2) to advanced oxygenated compounds is a promising strategy for the production of fuel and high-value chemicals, in which regulation of Co0-Co2+ active pairs is of great challenge to the performance of catalysts. In this work, Co-based catalysts supported on TiO2 modified by La and Al were designed and a novel Co metal-CoAl2O4 active structure was constructed through surface solid reaction to facilitate the higher alcohol synthesis (HAS) from syngas. The results show that the formation of CoAl2O4 by the interaction between Co and Al is conducive to maintaining the stability of Co2+ in catalyst and promoting the coupling of the intermediate CHx* and CO*. Besides, the promoters La and Al are uniformly dispersed on the surface of the catalyst, and inhibit the sintering of the active sites. As a result, the catalyst modified by Al and La exhibits the conversion rate of syngas almost three times as high as that of bare Co/TiO2 catalyst, and the selectivity of total alcohols reached 39.1% at 270 °C. A new feasible strategy of solid reaction on TiO2 provides an interesting way to create Co0-Co2+ active pairs for syngas conversion to higher alcohols.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">DOI: 10.1016/j.apsusc.2023.157330</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:label rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">publication1</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/ontologies/2023/11/new_onto.owl#publication10 -->

